From 56e219f8db8c68790314ed840e3818fdc669c69c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 01:35:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: choose which internal partitions are shown

With "Show the internal disks (read-only)" on, every partition of the
internal disks that has a file system was mounted read-only: also the
partitions of Windows and a disk of personal files that someone wants to
keep out of Kodi, of its add-ons and of file sharing, while the disks of
their films are shown.

Add "Internal partitions that are shown" under the switch, shown only
while it is on. It lists the partitions that this start sees, as
/usr/lib/libreelec/internal-partitions lists them, with their label, file
system, size and disk (the model, and the end of its serial number, which
tells two disks of one model apart), and the name of their device now.
The first line of the list is for any partition that is not in it (a new
one, another disk, another PC); with no list yet, everything is ticked, as
the system does today. What is not ticked is not mounted from the next
start. The choice is saved by the same script, which 95-udevil-mount.rules
asks before it mounts an internal partition; nothing of it is kept here.

A partition is told by the WWN and the serial number of its disk and its
identifier in the partition table, never by a name such as sda1. One that
cannot be told apart (a disk with no serial number, a partition with no
identifier) is not offered: it goes with the first line, and that is
said. A list that cannot be read is said, and offered again with nothing
ticked, which is what the system does with it (no internal partition is
mounted). In a start that hides the internal disks there is nothing to
list, and that is said. A list that is closed, or Kodi stopping, changes
nothing; a list that hides nothing is taken away.

The help of the item says what it does not do: a partition that is not
mounted is still on its disk, where a program that runs with full rights
could read it, and the way to keep every internal disk out of reach is to
turn the switch off. The text under the switch and the explanation shown
before it is turned on say that partitions can be chosen.

The script is run with a PATH and a locale of its own, not in the
environment of Kodi. The texts are in English, and in Spanish for
Argentina ("vos"), Spain and Mexico.
